  "frobenius_window_stacking": {
    "claim": "The symmetry-breaking windows V_i = <theta^(2^j)> (consecutive normal-basis blocks) satisfy pi^dim(V_i) = V_(i+1) and are disjoint. This is the GGMP section 3.1 mechanism: a summand from F_i = pi^((i-1)dim)(F_1) folds into F_1 as [lambda^((i-1)dim)]P', giving the m! saving with |F_1| unknowns. It needs only a normal basis, so it holds at n = 131.",
    "verified": {
      "n11_dim3_m3": true,
      "n131_dim4_m3": true,
      "n131_dim2_m3": true
    },
    "reproduce": "sage factor_base_dor.sage --frobenius-windows --field 131 --dim 4 --summands 3",
    "does_not_give": "The n-fold invariance saving. A window is not Frobenius-invariant, and on the prime subgroup Frobenius acts as lambda, so the Frobenius images of a relation are scalar multiples of it. GGMP Theorems 3.4/3.5 need each factor base to be Frobenius-invariant; at n = 131 the only invariant bases are non-linear sets (ONB weight classes, Couveignes-Lercier)."
  },
  "stacking_weight_classes": {
    "question": "Can the n-fold Frobenius-invariance saving and the m! symmetry-breaking saving stack where no invariant linear subspace exists (x^n-1 = (x+1)(irreducible), as at n = 131)?",
    "construction": "W_w = {x : normal-basis Hamming weight w}. Frobenius rotates normal-basis coordinates, so each W_w is invariant and orbits are rotation classes. Distinct weights give disjoint invariant classes, so P1 in W_a, P2 in W_b breaks symmetry while keeping the orbit saving.",
    "metric": "trials = (unknowns + 1) / coverage (relation collection); enumeration_work = trials * pairs_per_target (enumeration-oracle proxy)",
    "findings": [
      "Invariance saving is exactly n-fold in unknowns: n=11 44 abscissae -> 4 orbits; n=13 169 -> 13. Trials fall about 10-20x versus normal-basis windows.",
      "Breaking symmetry across distinct weight classes keeps the n-fold saving, halves coverage, and cuts pairs per target 2.7-3.9x; enumeration work falls 1.2x (n=11) to 1.9x (n=13) versus the symmetric scheme over the same union.",
      "Against windows, total enumeration work depends on base size: the weight-class base is larger, and at n=13 the windows need less raw work."
    ],
    "caveats": "Toy fields; m = 2; coverage over target abscissae without the cofactor/subgroup split; pairs_per_target is an enumeration proxy. Weight-class membership is a cardinality constraint, so it cannot be Weil-descended linearly for a Groebner oracle; per-trial SAT cost is not measured.",
